person  W5-PLAN §4.4, the step only a person can do (for 2026, then 2024):
person  In SolidWorks, open C:\Users\M0obo\Documents\GearGeneratorProbe\w7-full-20261115-000138\out-w6w5\a2\Reducer_12to1.SLDASM. Click gear G1 — the small gear on S1, at the origin — and drag it round its axis by about a quarter turn. Watch G4, the large gear on S3: it turns the same way as G1, much more slowly — a quarter turn of G1 turns G4 about 7.5°. G2 and G3, on S2, turn together the other way. No gear should leave its axis, slide along it, or pass through another. Then, in the Workbook window, press Update SolidWorks with nothing changed: the footer should say the gears stand where they were turned. Tell us what you saw, on each version.
person  W6-PLAN §6.1, the step only a person can do (for 2026, then 2024):
person  In C:\Users\M0obo\Documents\GearGeneratorProbe\w7-full-20261115-000138\out-w6w5\r1, open Bevel_Reducer.xlsx in the Workbook window and Bevel_Reducer.SLDASM in SolidWorks. Drag G1 — the small bevel gear on the horizontal shaft S1 — round its axis by about a quarter turn. G2, the large bevel gear on the upright shaft, turns half as far, and G3 under it with it; G4, the large spur gear on S3, turns a sixth as far as G1 and the other way round from G2. Nothing leaves its axis or passes through another gear. Then press Update SolidWorks with nothing changed: the footer says the gears stand where they were turned.
